{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01JwQ5os8EPwXSm8JEtfqCNF","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The file has been written at `/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/system-design/payments-system/answers/08-observability-analytics.md`. Here's a summary of what's covered across all 10 questions:\n\n---\n\n### What's in the file\n\n**Core Questions (Q1–Q7):**\n\n- **Q1** — Full checkout funnel instrumentation with a shared event envelope, specific event names (`checkout_started` → `payment_form_viewed` → `checkout_completed`), typed TypeScript interfaces, Mermaid funnel diagram, and a BigQuery SQL query for conversion rate per step.\n\n- **Q2** — Three distinct abandonment types (`pre_engagement`, `mid_entry`, `post_submit_failure`) modeled with `beforeunload` + Beacon API, a decision table mapping each type to product hypotheses, and notes on server-side reconciliation for mobile reliability.\n\n- **Q3** — Four-layer PCI sanitization strategy: Stripe's iframe boundary (can't read PAN by design), Sentry `beforeSend` with regex + key-blocklist scrubbing, Datadog RUM `defaultPrivacyLevel: 'mask'`, and an analytics allow-list pattern. Includes Luhn-check guidance and field-level redaction.\n\n- **Q4** — Custom `performance.mark()` / `performance.measure()` calls for `stripe_js_load` and `stripe_elements_mount`, reported as Datadog RUM custom vitals. Includes a BigQuery SQL funnel segmenting conversion by load time bucket (`fast/medium/slow/very_slow`).\n\n- **Q5** — Real-time dashboard architecture with StatsD metric definitions, full Datadog monitor YAML for P1/P2 alerting thresholds (90% critical, 93% warning), decline-by-code breakdown, 3DS funnel tracking, and segmentation by country/device/payment method. Includes a volume-gate caveat for low-traffic hours.\n\n- **Q6** — Retry instrumentation with `submission_attempt` counter, `previous_decline_code` chaining, a `checkout_retry_action` event for CTA clicks, a metrics table (retry success rate, time-to-retry, CTA click rate), and a BigQuery recovery rate query segmented by decline code. Covers soft vs. hard decline distinction.\n\n- **Q7** — Risk framework for `payment_intent_id` in analytics: data linkage, third-party data sharing, PCI scope expansion, and legal subpoena exposure. Decision table (your warehouse ✅, Sentry ✅, GA4 ❌) with a SHA-256 hashing approach for safe correlation IDs when third-party inclusion is necessary.\n\n**Deep Questions (Q8–Q10):**\n\n- **Q8** — Full experiment design for diagnosing whether 4-second Elements load causes 23% abandonment. Covers instrumentation for 4 hypotheses (load time, price shock, account friction, trust signals), observational confound analysis SQL, a 4-variant A/B test design (Mermaid diagram), sample size reasoning, and segmented analysis approach.\n\n- **Q9** — End-to-end observability architecture (Mermaid diagram), error layer classification function (`stripe_client` / `your_api` / `network`), three specific dashboard panels with exact Datadog metric queries, supplementary panels, and full alerting YAML for 60-second incident detection.\n\n- **Q10** — Diagnostic playbook as a Mermaid decision tree, step-by-step investigation (deploy timestamp correlation, Stripe status API, Sentry release filtering, RUM version comparison, APM 5xx check, decline code distribution SQL, Stripe Dashboard navigation), rollback decision criteria table, and a feature-flag rollback command example."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a4d2bdf8f2a61e4a0 (use SendMessage with to: 'a4d2bdf8f2a61e4a0' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 36222\ntool_uses: 5\nduration_ms: 279419</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6f204bf-32e1-4fe7-87b7-580f13b36e45","uuid":"ca21218c-b9ba-43ee-8afe-e6b472e24ab7","timestamp":"2026-06-20T19:33:06.251Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Write a comprehensive answer file at `/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/system-design/payments-system/answers/08-observability-analytics.md` for Section 8: Observability & Analytics of a payments-system frontend system design study guide.\n\n## Section context\n\nThe section has 7 Core questions and 3 Deep questions. Answer EVERY question with interview-depth answers using this format for each:\n\n1. **Problem framing** — restate the question and why it matters\n2. **Approach** — your solution, mechanisms, code snippets or Mermaid diagrams where helpful\n3. **Tradeoffs** — alternatives considered and why you chose this path\n\n## Questions to answer\n\n### Core\n\n1. What events would you instrument in a checkout funnel to measure conversion at each step and identify drop-off points? Give specific event names, properties, and firing conditions.\n\n2. How do you track \"payment abandonment\" — distinguishing between: (a) user who leaves before entering any payment details, (b) user who starts entering card info but exits, and (c) user who clicks \"Pay\" but the request fails? Why does this distinction matter for product decisions?\n\n3. What is your strategy for tracking errors in a payment flow without accidentally logging sensitive card data (PANs, CVVs) to your analytics or error monitoring service? What specific fields or patterns do you sanitize before sending to Sentry or Datadog?\n\n4. How do you measure the performance of Stripe.js initialization and Elements mount time in RUM data? What custom performance marks would you use, and how do you correlate slow load times with conversion drop-off?\n\n5. Describe how you would build a real-time payment success rate dashboard. What metrics do you track (success rate, decline rate broken down by decline code, 3DS completion rate), and what is your alerting threshold that should page an on-call engineer?\n\n6. How do you instrument the retry behavior after a card decline to understand whether your retry UX is effective? What metrics tell you the retry flow is working vs. causing additional user frustration?\n\n7. What is the risk of sending `payment_intent_id` or `charge_id` in analytics events (e.g., to Google Analytics or Amplitude), and how do you evaluate whether to include these identifiers?\n\n### Deep\n\n8. You notice 23% of users who reach the payment step never click \"Pay\" — they simply leave. Your checkout form has a 4-second load time for Stripe Elements on mobile. Walk through how you would design and execute an experiment to determine whether the Elements load time is causally responsible for the abandonment rate, versus other hypotheses (price shock, trust signals, required account creation). What instrumentation do you add and how do you isolate the variable?\n\n9. Design an observability system for a payments frontend that gives you the following within 60 seconds of a production incident: (1) the percentage of checkout attempts failing vs. succeeding in real time, (2) whether the failure is in the Stripe client SDK, your own API, or the network between them, and (3) which user segments (by country, payment method, device type) are most affected. Describe the events, aggregation pipeline, and dashboard panels you would build.\n\n10. Your payment success rate drops from 94% to 87% after a deploy. You need to determine whether this is a frontend regression or a backend/Stripe issue. Describe your diagnostic playbook: which logs and metrics do you look at first, how do you correlate the deploy timestamp with the metric drop, how do you check whether Stripe has an ongoing incident, and what is your rollback decision criteria? Assume you have Datadog RUM, Sentry, and Stripe's Dashboard available.\n\n## Format requirements\n\n- Start with: `# Section 8: Observability & Analytics`\n- One `## Q1`, `## Q2`, … heading per question\n- Use bold for **Problem framing**, **Approach**, **Tradeoffs** subsections\n- Include Mermaid diagrams (in ```mermaid blocks) in the Approach where helpful\n- Include concrete code snippets where useful (TypeScript/JS)\n- Answers should be interview-depth (thorough but not exhaustive) — around 300-500 words per answer\n- Use specific metric names, event names, and tooling references (Datadog RUM, Sentry, OpenTelemetry, etc.)\n\nWrite the file now.\n","agentId":"a4d2bdf8f2a61e4a0","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The file has been written at `/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/system-design/payments-system/answers/08-observability-analytics.md`. Here's a summary of what's covered across all 10 questions:\n\n---\n\n### What's in the file\n\n**Core Questions (Q1–Q7):**\n\n- **Q1** — Full checkout funnel instrumentation with a shared event envelope, specific event names (`checkout_started` → `payment_form_viewed` → `checkout_completed`), typed TypeScript interfaces, Mermaid funnel diagram, and a BigQuery SQL query for conversion rate per step.\n\n- **Q2** — Three distinct abandonment types (`pre_engagement`, `mid_entry`, `post_submit_failure`) modeled with `beforeunload` + Beacon API, a decision table mapping each type to product hypotheses, and notes on server-side reconciliation for mobile reliability.\n\n- **Q3** — Four-layer PCI sanitization strategy: Stripe's iframe boundary (can't read PAN by design), Sentry `beforeSend` with regex + key-blocklist scrubbing, Datadog RUM `defaultPrivacyLevel: 'mask'`, and an analytics allow-list pattern. Includes Luhn-check guidance and field-level redaction.\n\n- **Q4** — Custom `performance.mark()` / `performance.measure()` calls for `stripe_js_load` and `stripe_elements_mount`, reported as Datadog RUM custom vitals. Includes a BigQuery SQL funnel segmenting conversion by load time bucket (`fast/medium/slow/very_slow`).\n\n- **Q5** — Real-time dashboard architecture with StatsD metric definitions, full Datadog monitor YAML for P1/P2 alerting thresholds (90% critical, 93% warning), decline-by-code breakdown, 3DS funnel tracking, and segmentation by country/device/payment method. Includes a volume-gate caveat for low-traffic hours.\n\n- **Q6** — Retry instrumentation with `submission_attempt` counter, `previous_decline_code` chaining, a `checkout_retry_action` event for CTA clicks, a metrics table (retry success rate, time-to-retry, CTA click rate), and a BigQuery recovery rate query segmented by decline code. Covers soft vs. hard decline distinction.\n\n- **Q7** — Risk framework for `payment_intent_id` in analytics: data linkage, third-party data sharing, PCI scope expansion, and legal subpoena exposure. Decision table (your warehouse ✅, Sentry ✅, GA4 ❌) with a SHA-256 hashing approach for safe correlation IDs when third-party inclusion is necessary.\n\n**Deep Questions (Q8–Q10):**\n\n- **Q8** — Full experiment design for diagnosing whether 4-second Elements load causes 23% abandonment. Covers instrumentation for 4 hypotheses (load time, price shock, account friction, trust signals), observational confound analysis SQL, a 4-variant A/B test design (Mermaid diagram), sample size reasoning, and segmented analysis approach.\n\n- **Q9** — End-to-end observability architecture (Mermaid diagram), error layer classification function (`stripe_client` / `your_api` / `network`), three specific dashboard panels with exact Datadog metric queries, supplementary panels, and full alerting YAML for 60-second incident detection.\n\n- **Q10** — Diagnostic playbook as a Mermaid decision tree, step-by-step investigation (deploy timestamp correlation, Stripe status API, Sentry release filtering, RUM version comparison, APM 5xx check, decline code distribution SQL, Stripe Dashboard navigation), rollback decision criteria table, and a feature-flag rollback command example."}],"resolvedModel":"blackboxai/anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5","totalDurationMs":279419,"totalTokens":36222,"totalToolUseCount":5,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":88,"cache_read_input_tokens":35248,"output_tokens":885,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"servi